  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 415 AM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nLong range forecast philosophy remains consistent with a wet and \nconvective weather pattern in place. The W Atl ridge axis pushes \nsouth and moves closer to, or even over SoFlo, basically reducing \nwinds to a light sout-southwest flow. Periods of calm winds are also \npossible even during the afternoon hours, with sea breeze boundaries \nbecoming the focal point for deeper convection.  \n\nNBM and long-range guidance remain very aggressive with POPs across \nthe area, with max values in the 80-90% range through the weekend, \nand still lingering the 70-80% range early next week. PWATs, dew \npoints, rainfall estimates all remain above or well above seasonal \nnormals through the long term. Best chances for strong convection \nwill happen along the East coast sea breeze around the Atlantic \nmetro areas early in the afternoon, then pushing inland during the \nlate afternoon hours. In addition, outflow boundary interactions may \nspark late afternoon/evening storms over the metro areas at times. \nHazards will include gusty winds, heavy rainfall and frequent \nlightning.\n\nTemperatures are expected to remain seasonably hot each day, with \nafternoon highs in the low to mid 90s. Overnight lows should remain \nin the low 80s across the coastlines, and mid 70s over the interior.",
